-- PREFABS: the things the world can be given a copy of, by name, and what each is made of (#191, step 7;
-- src/engine/procgen/prefab.h).
--
-- A line is a prefab: its name, the model it shows (a name in models.lua), and what a copy carries.
--
--     name = {
--       model = "crane",
--       physics = { body = "static", friction = 0.7 },   -- solid: the model's collision shapes become bodies
--       draw = { class = "structure", distance = 400 },      -- how far it is drawn: a draw class, or metres
--       script = "crane_swing.lua",                         -- a behaviour every copy runs (it moves the model's nodes)
--       uses = { { at = "handle", say = "open", back = "close", move = { door = 105 }, speed = 110 }, ... },
--                 what a person standing at a copy can do (E): at a marker of the model, move parts to a value and
--                 back, run parts on and on (`run`, with `seconds` to stop by itself), or sit (`sit = true`).
--                 `sway` rocks parts while it is on; `coast` lets running parts slow to a stop; a `*` in `at`
--                 makes the use once for every marker that fits (a door for every handle). A use that sits is
--                 on while the person stays, so its `run` or `sway` lasts that long (a bike is pedalled).
--                 A use may have an `id`; another may say `needs = "<id>"` (offered only while that one is on: a
--                 server slides out only with the rack's door open) or `unless = "<id>"` (only while it is off).
--                 src/engine/procgen/prefab.h says each word. A use whose marker or part the model lacks is left out.
--     }
--
-- A level places one with an entity: { "shape": "prefab", "prefab": "crane", "position": [x, y, z] }.
-- Every copy shares the one model. A new kind of thing is a new line. No C++ changes.
return {
